1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,350
Desert Animal Survival.

2
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:14,637
Deserts present extreme physiological challenges: surface temperatures can exceed 60°C, annual rainfall is often below 250 millimetres, and water sources are unpredictable.

3
00:00:14,637 --> 00:00:22,925
Animals inhabiting these environments have evolved specialised adaptations for thermoregulation, water conservation, and locomotion.

4
00:00:22,925 --> 00:00:32,500
The fennec fox (Vulpes zerda), the smallest canid species, dissipates heat through its disproportionately large ears — up to 15 centimetres across.

5
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:40,750
These ears are richly supplied with blood vessels that release heat through radiation, helping maintain body temperature below the lethal threshold.

6
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:54,562
Conversely, the Saharan silver ant (Cataglyphis bombycina) has triangular-section body hairs that reflect near-infrared and visible light, keeping its body several degrees cooler than the surrounding air while foraging during peak heat.

7
00:00:54,562 --> 00:01:00,487
The kangaroo rat (Dipodomys species) of North American deserts never drinks water.

8
00:01:00,487 --> 00:01:11,600
It obtains metabolic water from dry seeds and produces urine so concentrated that osmolarity reaches 5,500 mOsm/L — roughly five times that of human urine.

9
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:18,337
Nasal passages contain countercurrent heat exchangers that recover up to 90% of water from exhaled air.

10
00:01:18,337 --> 00:01:29,837
A 2020 study in Physiological and Biochemical Zoology documented that kangaroo rats reduce evaporative water loss by lowering metabolic rate by 30% during acute dehydration.

11
00:01:29,837 --> 00:01:37,037
The thorny devil (Moloch horridus), a lizard native to Australian deserts, collects water through capillary action.

12
00:01:37,037 --> 00:01:46,012
Its skin is covered with microscopic grooves between scales that channel water toward the mouth, allowing it to drink from morning dew or moisture in sand.

